ap:253063.body:
About UM6P:
Mohammed VI Polytechnic University (UM6P) is an internationally oriented institution of higher learning, that is committed to an educational system based on the highest standards of teaching and research in fields related to the sustainable economic development of Morocco and Africa. UM6P is an institution oriented towards applied research and innovation. On a specific focus on Africa, UM6P aims to position these fields as the forefront and become a university of international standing.
More than just a traditional academic institution, UM6P is a platform for experimentation and a pool of opportunities, for students, professors and staff. It offers a high-quality living and study environment thanks to its state-of-the-art infrastructure. With an innovative approach, UM6P places research and innovation at the heart of its educational project as a driving force of a business model.
In its research approach, the UM6P promotes transdisciplinary, entrepreneurship spirit and collaboration with internal and external institutions for developing up to date scientific research and at continent level in order to address real challenges.
All our programs run as start-ups and can be self-organized when they reach a critical mass. Thus, academic liberty is promoted as far as funding is developed by research teams.
The research programs are integrated from long-term research to short-term applications in linkage with incubation and start-up ecosystems.
About CBS:
The Chemical & Biochemical Sciences Green Process Engineering Department (CBS) is a component of the Mohammed VI Polytechnic University (UM6P). The main objective of CBS is to set up a distinctive research-teaching program, of international level, to meet the research and teaching challenges of UM6P, in particular on green and environmental chemistry applied to all aspects of chemical sciences: organic and inorganic chemistry, analytical chemistry, chemical biology, biochemical and thermal reactions.
Research at CBS is organized around several major areas, which aim to answer challenging industrial questions, from complex chemical and biochemical reactions to scale-up and validation of process engineering. CBS projects aim at an in-depth understanding of the molecular mechanisms of all transformations in order to propose new original alternatives in terms of efficiency, environmental friendliness and sustainability.
Background
The treatment of water and industrial effluents requires the development of efficient, selective, and sustainable separation processes. These processes enable the removal of impurities, the recovery of valuable elements, and the reuse of treated water.
This postdoctoral project focuses on developing, optimizing, and combining various separation technologies, particularly membrane processes, liquid–liquid extraction, and resin-based processes.
Responsibilities
The postdoctoral researcher will contribute to the following activities:
Develop and optimize separation processes for the treatment of water and industrial effluents;
Investigate various technologies, including membrane processes, liquid-liquid extraction, ion-exchange resins, adsorption, and other separation processes;
Select appropriate materials, membranes, resins, and solvents according to the targeted impurities;
Plan and conduct experiments at the laboratory and/or pilot scale;
Optimize the operating parameters affecting process selectivity and efficiency;
Characterize feed solutions and the different streams obtained after separation;
Evaluate the performance, stability, regeneration, and reusability of separation materials;
Propose hybrid configurations combining multiple separation processes;
Contribute to process design, equipment sizing, and scale-up;
Prepare technical reports, scientific publications, and conference presentations;
Contribute to student supervision and the research activities of the team.

Candidate Profile
PhD in Process Engineering, Chemical Engineering, or a related field;
Strong knowledge of unit operations and separation processes;
Expertise in one or more of the following technologies: Membrane processes, including reverse osmosis, nanofiltration, ultrafiltration, electrodialysis, or related technologies;
Liquid–liquid extraction;
Ion-exchange or adsorbent resins;
Adsorption, precipitation, crystallization, or hybrid processes;

Experience in water treatment, desalination, or industrial effluent treatment would be considered an asset;
Strong skills in the analysis and interpretation of experimental results;
Good scientific writing skills in both English and French;
Ability to work independently and as part of a team, with strong analytical skills, rigor, and attention to detail.

Duration
One year
Location
CBS Department-UM6P, Jorf Lasfar, Morocco
Application
The application must include the following in a single pdf file:
A detailed curriculum vitae;
A cover letter;
A copy of the PhD diploma;
A list of scientific publications;
Contact details of two professional or academic referees.

ap:253114.body:
Faculty of Humanities, Leiden University Centre for Linguistics

PhD candidate, Dutch morphology and constructicography
Starting date: January 2027 (a later starting date can be negotiated)

Deadline : November 1, 2026

Description

Leiden University Centre for Linguistics (LUCL) invites applications for a fully funded PhD position within the NWO project ‘The Morphicon: A new gateway to morphological knowledge’.

The PhD in this project will work in a small team to build a morphological constructicon for Dutch and use it to investigate the division of labour between grammatical regularity and lexical idiosyncrasy.

Constructicons are digital networks of grammatical constructions in a language. Such resources are being built for a variety of major languages, but not yet for Dutch. Moreover, existing constructicons all focus on syntactic constructions, neglecting morphology. The aim of this project is to develop the first morphological constructicon or “Morphicon” with Dutch as the target language. The resource will be developed by a team of three researchers—the PI, a Postdoc and the PhD—in collaboration with the Instituut voor de Nederlandse Taal.

The PhD will participate in the team effort, but will also have their own research project.

The team will work together on transforming existing lexicographic and grammatical resources into a digital network. The network will consist of lexical constructions (simple and complex words) and grammatical constructions (word-formation patterns), linked to one another by the properties they share. An important part of the work will be to find, test and develop digital tools to assist in classification, annotation and network building.

The specific research project of the PhD will target the interplay between grammatical regularity and lexical idiosyncrasy in morphological constructions. To this aim, the PhD will investigate a set of selected constructional families in greater depth to assess the proportion and nature of idiosyncrasy within each family.

This PhD position is part of an NWO-funded Open Competition Large project involving PI dr. Jenny Audring (Leiden University) and external partner prof. dr. Sjef Barbiers (Instituut voor de Nederlandse Taal).

Tasks and responsibilities

Extracting word-formation patterns from the existing descriptive literature and encoding them in systematic constructional schemas;
Matching complex words to the appropriate schemas and subschemas and annotating them in such a way that the inventory of constructions can be transformed into a network;
Assisting in finding, testing and evaluating digital tools, including LLMs, to aid classification, annotation and network building;
Investigating Dutch morphological constructions quantitatively and qualitatively;
Presenting (joint) research results at workshops and conferences;
Publishing (joint) research results and writing a PhD-thesis within the period of appointment;
(Assisting in) teaching activities at bachelor level ;
Participating in (the organisation of) knowledge dissemination activities;
Participating in meetings and training sessions of the project research group.

Selection criteria

A Master’s degree in linguistics, completed before the start date;
Demonstrable interest in grammar, especially morphology, from a descriptive and/or theoretical perspective;
Native or near-native proficiency of Dutch and a good command of academic English;
Excellent research and analytical skills;
Experience in computational linguistics and/or affinity with the framework of Construction Grammar is a plus.

Terms and conditions

Appointment will be according to the terms of the Collective Labour Agreement of Dutch Universities (CAO Nederlandse Universiteiten). The employment will be for a period of four years. The first contract will be for 12 months with an extension for the following 36 months contingent on performance and compatibility. You are expected to work in Leiden and t he appointment must lead to the completion of a PhD thesis.

Salary range from € 3.204.- gross per month in the first year for a fulltime appointment to € 4,051.- in the fourth year (pay scale for PhDs, in accordance with the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities), plus substantial additional holiday and year-end allowances. Candidates from outside the Netherlands may be eligible for a substantial tax break.
Starting date: January 2027; a later starting date can be negotiated.

Research at our faculty/institute

Leiden University Centre for Linguistics ( LUCL ) is one of seven Institutes that falls under the Faculty of Humanities. With over 120 linguists working at the Institute, it is a rich research environment that prides itself on fostering scientific excellence. At LUCL we offer extensive research support to our researchers.

LUCL has a longstanding tradition in the study of the world’s languages and features unique linguistic expertise. Current theoretical insights are combined with modern experimental methods in its research profile area ‘Language Diversity in the World’. Researchers from our  six umbrella research groups  are experts in their respective fields while finding innovative ways of collaborating with colleagues in other fields and disciplines.

The Faculty of Humanities at Leiden University is a unique international centre for the advanced study of languages, cultures, arts, and societies worldwide, in their historical contexts from prehistory to the present.  Our faculty is home to more than 6,000 students and 800 staff members. For more information see: https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/en/humanities.

Diversity

Fostering an inclusive community is a central element of the values and vision of Leiden University. Leiden University is committed to becoming an inclusive community which enables all students and staff to feel valued and respected and to develop their full potential. Diversity in experiences and perspectives enriches our teaching and strengthens our research. High quality teaching and research is inclusive.

Questions?

For further information on the research project and the PhD position please contact the PI, dr. Jenny Audring, [email protected]
For practical questions, please contact the Institute Manager, Maarit van Gammeren: [email protected] .

Applications

Applications should be submitted no later than 1 November, 2026 via the blue button in our application system. Your application should include the following information:

A cover letter, in which you explain your motivation;
A full academic CV;
A list of all Bachelor and Master-level modules you have taken, with an official transcript of grades;
A writing sample, preferably your MA thesis and an article or term paper that you wrote that pertains to the topic of the position;
The names and email addresses of two referees, including your MA thesis supervisor(s), who may be approached by the selection committee (no reference letters).

The vacancy closes on 1 November, 2026. Interviews will take place in the second half of November 2026.

Enquiries from agencies are not appreciated.

ap:253121.body:
IT auditor

Zit jij altijd vol met ideeën voor verbetering en wil je bouwen aan een toekomstbestendig en realistisch IT-audit programma en wil je dit doen binnen een uitdagende maatschappelijk context en inspirerende en lerende organisatie? Dan zijn wij op zoek naar jou!

Het Bestuursbureau van de Universiteit Leiden is op zoek naar een  IT Auditor (0,8 - 1,0 FTE).

Dit ga je doen

Je draagt bij aan het opstellen en uitvoeren van een organisatie brede Interne Audit planning. Je bereidt in nauwe samenwerking met de lijnorganisatie IT-audits voor en voert ze op een efficiënte en effectieve wijze uit om zo een aantoonbare toegevoegde waarde te bieden aan de organisatie. Dit doe je door een zorgvuldig, objectief en onafhankelijk oordeel of advies te formuleren over onze IT-systemen en gerelateerde processen. Je monitort of de aanbevelingen en adviezen worden geïmplementeerd in de organisatie.

Jouw belangrijkste taken en verantwoordelijkheden zijn:
Verantwoordelijk voor én meewerken aan de planning, scoping en uitvoering van audits naar IT-processen binnen de Universiteit Leiden.
Bespreken van audit bevindingen met het verantwoordelijke management en hen overtuigen van de noodzaak tot risicobeheersing.
Op vasthoudende én realistische wijze monitoren van de opvolging van de aanbevelingen.
Het bewaken en verbeteren van de kwaliteit van de audits en daarmee de IT-audit functie naar een hoger plan brengen.
Het volgen van de ontwikkelingen op het vakgebied om een goede bijdrage te kunnen leveren aan de kwaliteitsverbeteringen en om deze te vertalen naar onze eigen organisatie.

Hier ga je werken

Het Bestuursbureau ondersteunt het College van Bestuur en draagt zorg voor de voorbereiding van beleid en de uitvoering van besluiten. Hierbij onderhoudt het Bestuursbureau nauwe relaties met faculteiten en expertisecentra. Op een aantal terreinen zoals financiën, personeel, juridische zaken en strategische communicatie heeft het Bestuursbureau ook een serviceverlenende rol voor de gehele organisatie. Verdere informatie over het Bestuursbureau kun je vinden op de website .

De afdeling Audit maakt organisatorisch onderdeel uit van het Bestuursbureau, maar functioneert onafhankelijk. De afdeling rapporteert aan het College van Bestuur en werkt nauw samen met de externe accountant. De afdeling Audit is verantwoordelijk voor het uitvoeren van audits op processen bij faculteiten en ondersteunende eenheden, beoordeelt daarbij de effectiviteit en efficiëntie en geeft adviezen voor verbetering. Audit helpt de Universiteit Leiden haar ambities waar te maken en haar financiële waarden te beschermen. Wij bereiken impact door vanuit een onafhankelijke positie het management proactief een spiegel voor te houden, waar nodig uit te dagen en te stimuleren de interne risicobeheersing te versterken.

Wat doet de afdeling?
Financial audit : (accountants)controle naar de getrouwheid en rechtmatigheid van financiële verantwoordingsinformatie;
Operational audit : het onderzoeken van en adviseren over kwaliteitsaspecten van processen en daarmee samenhangende informatievoorziening;
Compliance audit : het onderzoeken van en adviseren over de naleving van wet- en regelgeving;
IT audit : het onderzoeken van en adviseren over de effectiviteit van IT in de organisatie en de IT organisatie in het waarborgen van vertrouwelijkheid, integriteit en beschikbaarheid van informatie;
Over i ge aan de controle verwante werkzaamheden .

Het auditteam
Het team bestaat uit 5 enthousiaste collega's met diverse expertises (waaronder RA), leeftijden en ervaringsniveaus. Als afdeling richten wij ons op alle belangrijke processen en geldstromen binnen de Universiteit Leiden. Wij werken als team op een informele wijze nauw samen. Daarbij heeft iedereen veel zelfstandigheid en vrijheid in zijn of haar rol en alle ruimte om (actief) mee te denken over een verdere verbetering van de manier waarop wij als team werken en onze taken uitvoeren. Wij bieden een mooie en inspirerende uitdaging om te bouwen aan een realistisch en toekomstbestendig IT-audit programma.

Dit breng je mee

In dit team zoeken we iemand die zelfstandig aan de slag kan. Elke functie is anders dus er is in het begin aandacht voor inwerken. Deze functie past bij jou als je je herkent in de volgende punten:
Aantoonbaar acteren op WO werk- en denkniveau;
Bij voorkeur een afgeronde RE opleiding (eventueel aangevuld met certificering als CISA, CISSP);
Kennis van AFAS, privacywetgeving, informatiebeveiliging en cybercrime zijn een pré;
Kennis van IT Governance, nieuwe ontwikkelingen, en technologieën (AVG, Cloud, AI);
Onderzoekend en analytisch ingesteld en affiniteit met beheers aspecten van IT-technologie en (ontwikkel)processen;
Kritisch durven zijn waarbij sensitiviteit aanwezig is voor verhoudingen binnen de organisatieonderdelen;
Vernieuwend, zelfstandig en een goede dosis eigenaarschap;
Communicatief sterk, zowel verbaal als schriftelijk, waarmee je met overtuiging en in het belang van de klant je boodschap kunt overdragen;
Een teamplayer en doorzetter.

Herken je jezelf volledig in dit profiel óf voldoe jij niet aan alle eisen maar denk je wel dat dit de functie voor jou is? Dan nodigen wij je graag uit om te solliciteren!

Dit bieden we je

Een leuke uitdagende functie met veel ruimte voor eigen inbreng en ontwikkeling in een inspirerende werkomgeving. Aanstelling vindt plaats conform de CAO Nederlandse Universiteiten. Het betreft een tijdelijke aanstelling voor de duur van één jaar. Bij goed functioneren en het voldoen aan de functie-eisen wordt de tijdelijke aanstelling omgezet in een dienstverband voor onbepaalde tijd. Het salaris bedraagt, afhankelijk van opleiding en werkervaring, minimaal € 5.932,- en maximaal € 7.573,- bruto per maand bij een fulltime aanstelling (salarisschaal 12).

Daarnaast bieden we je:
Vakantietoeslag (8%), een eindejaarsuitkering (8,3%) en een aantrekkelijke pensioenregeling bij het ABP;

Volledige vergoeding van reiskosten met OV voor woon-werkreizen.

Flexibele werkduur: standaard heb je minimaal 29 vakantiedagen bij een fulltime werkweek van 38 uur; je kunt daarnaast extra verlof opbouwen door bijvoorbeeld fulltime 40 uur per week te werken en zo 96 extra verlofuren op te bouwen óf door 96 verlofuren in te leveren en een werkweek van 36 uur te werken;

Ruime keuze in secundaire arbeidsvoorwaarden; zo is het bespreekbaar om voor een sabbatical of betaald ouderschapsverlof te kiezen. Binnen ons keuzemodel arbeidsvoorwaarden kunnen vrije dagen en/of salaris ingezet worden voor bijvoorbeeld een voordelig sportabonnement en fietsregeling;

Als de werkzaamheden het toelaten is hybride werken binnen Nederland mogelijk;

Een thuiswerkvergoeding (dag- en internetvergoeding) en aandacht voor goede werkplekken, daarnaast krijg je een laptop en een mobiele telefoon van de universiteit tot je beschikking.

Meer informatie over arbeidsvoorwaarden lees je hier:  https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/werken-bij/sollicitatieprocedure-en-arbeidsvoorwaarden

Dit vinden wij belangrijk

Het bevorderen van een inclusieve gemeenschap staat centraal in de waarden en visie van de Universiteit Leiden. We streven ernaar om een open gemeenschap te zijn waar alle studenten en medewerkers zich gewaardeerd en gerespecteerd voelen en zich volop kunnen ontplooien. Diversiteit in ervaringen en perspectieven verrijkt ons onderwijs en versterkt ons onderzoek. Een inclusieve academische omgeving waarin studenten en medewerkers hun ervaringen en opvattingen kunnen delen, zien we als essentieel voor de ontwikkeling van talenten.

Solliciteren of meer weten?

Direct solliciteren? Klik dan op de sollicitatiebutton.

Wil je eerst nog meer informatie over de inhoud van deze functie? Neem contact op met Waqas Mir, Hoofd Audit, [email protected] , 0653654809. Solliciteren kan tot en met 5 november 2026. Je ontvangt uiterlijk twee weken na de sluitingsdatum van de vacature een reactie op je sollicitatie.

Om elkaar goed te leren kennen, volgen we een aantal stappen in de sollicitatieprocedure. Meer informatie lees je hier: https://www.universiteitleiden.nl/werken-bij/sollicitatieprocedure-en-arbeidsvoorwaarden

Je woont en bent officieel ingeschreven als inwoner van Nederland of bent bereid te verhuizen zodra je de functie aanvaardt.
Een pre-employment screening (referenties, diploma’s, VOG) kan onderdeel uitmaken van de selectieprocedure.

Acquisitie naar aanleiding van deze vacature wordt niet op prijs gesteld. Mocht je ons toch cv’s toesturen dan kunnen daar geen rechten aan worden ontleend. #LI-Hybrid

ap:253283.body:
Job posting Period:  22-09-2026 to 30-10-2026
Position Number:  22349
The Graduate School of International Studies (École supérieure d’études internationales – ESEI) at Université Laval, located in Quebec City (Canada), is a centre of excellence in multidisciplinary research and teaching on international affaires. ESEI is currently seeking applications for a faculty position on Arctic defence and security.
The competition is open to applicants from all disciplines relevant to the study of defence and security dynamics in the Arctic. Applications from scholars in economics, law, geography, history, management, political science, and other relevant disciplines are particularly encouraged.
The successful candidate will hold the  Desgagnés Chair in Arctic Defence and Security  and will have access to a research budget of more than $200,000 per year for five years.
The Chair’s mandate is to conduct leading-edge interdisciplinary research. In particular, it seeks to examine transformations in the Arctic strategic environment in a context marked by the return of great-power rivalry and by rapidly evolving economic, legal, geographic, and political realities in the region.
By bringing academic expertise to strategic issues of primary importance to Canada, the Chair seeks to contribute actively to national debates on defence and security.
Research conducted through the Chair may address, among other topics:
The economic and geoeconomic dimensions of competition in the Arctic;
The costs, choices, and trade-offs associated with defence and security policies in the region;
The legal frameworks and governance issues that shape activities in the Arctic, particularly in a region where different and overlapping areas of state jurisdiction coexist;
The historical and territorial transformations of the region and their implications for contemporary security issues;
Transformations in the strategic environment and power relations in the Arctic;
The resilience and security of critical northern infrastructure, particularly land and maritime transportation infrastructure;
Maritime security and the evolution of commercial shipping;
The development and transportation of natural resources and their strategic implications.

A research advisor will support the Chairholder in developing, planning, and submitting research grant applications, while also helping to maximize the Chair’s outreach and engagement activities. Funding will also be available to offer doctoral scholarships associated with the Chair.
The successful candidate will join the Graduate School of International Studies as a regular faculty member starting June 1, 2027, and no later than September 1, 2027. Hiring may be at any academic rank in accordance with the   Collective Agreementbetween Laval University and the Syndicat des professeurs et des professeures de   l’Université Laval (SPUL).
Duties include:
Teaching at the graduate level;
Supervising master’s essays, dissertations and doctoral theses;
Conducting research and publishing on Arctic defence and security;
Participating in School-related pedagogical and administrative activities;
Being involved in any other activity provided for in the Université Laval-SPUL collective agreement;

Selection criteria:
Ability to teach in French within three years of appointment.
Hold a PhD in a field relevant to Arctic defence and security;
Applications from doctoral candidates nearing completion of their PhD will be considered, provided that a letter from the department chair confirms the expected date of submission or defense;
Demonstrate strong commitment to pluri-/interdisciplinarity, whether through academic background, research program, teaching experience, or collaborative projects;
An excellent research record, with publications in top peer-reviewed journals in the candidate’s field, or strong potential to develop such a record for early-career applicants;
University-level teaching experience;
A proven track record of securing research funding from recognized granting agencies, or strong potential to obtain such funding in the near future;
Ability to teach a graduate-level methodology course.
Proven integration into national and international research networks is considered an asset.

Employment conditions:
Regular full-time position;
Salary based on recognized experience with benefits in accordance with the Université Laval-SPUL collective agreement (available in French only)  https://www.rh.ulaval.ca/personnel-enseignant-et-derecherche/professeurs/conventions-collectives

Applications:
Application deadline: October 30, 2026
Starting date: June 1, 2027 (no later than September 1, 2027).

The application file must include:
A cover letter demonstrating the candidate’s commitment to pluri/interdisciplinarity and international studies;
A curriculum vitae;
A document of no more than two pages outlining a proposed five-year research chair program;
A sample publication.

Valuing equity, diversity, and excellence, Université Laval is firmly committed to providing all its staff with a work and living environment characterized by an inclusive culture. For Université Laval, diversity is a source of strength, and we encourage qualified individuals of all origins, genders, sexual orientations, gender identities or expressions, as well as individuals with disabilities, to apply.
Université Laval also adheres to an   employment equity program  for women, members of visible or ethnic minorities, Indigenous peoples, and individuals with disabilities. To this end, all applicants will be invited to complete a confidential self-identification form upon submission of their application.
All applications will be processed confidentially. Applications must be sent to:
Jonathan Paquin
Director
Graduate School of International Studies
Université Laval
[email protected]

ap:253353.body:
The Graduate School of International Studies ( l’École supérieure d’études internationales – ESEI) at Université Laval, located in Québec City, Canada, is a centre of excellence in multidisciplinary research and teaching on international issues.
ESEI is currently seeking a faculty member whose research focuses on global inequalities and trade. The successful candidate will hold the Research Chair in Global Inequalities and Trade .
The competition is open to candidates from all disciplines relevant to this field. Applications from economics, law, geography, history, sociology, management, political science, and other relevant disciplines are particularly encouraged.
International trade structures power relations among developed countries, emerging economies, and least developed countries, as well as power dynamics among actors within societies. It has a direct impact on the lives of hundreds of millions of people and on their aspirations for a more equitable world. The Chair’s research will examine the reciprocal relationships between the governance of international economic and diplomatic relations, on the one hand, and inequalities, on the other, in the context of the reconfiguration of the emerging world order.
The Chair’s research may address, but is not limited to, the following issues: the role of financial institutions in addressing global economic inequalities (e.g., sovereign debt restructuring); the links between international aid, export-led growth, and human development; the role of sovereign wealth funds and the financialization of the economy in the unequal distribution of wealth and democratic backsliding; the impact of market opening and multinational corporations on local populations; and the preservation of an inclusive multilateral trading system.
In addition to its research activities, the Chair will develop an innovative teaching program based on simulations of negotiations and coordinated action within international organizations working in the fields of international development and trade (IMF, UNDP, WTO, etc.). These initiatives will raise students’ awareness of the importance of multilateral cooperation in reducing inequalities among countries.
A research advisor will support the Chairholder in developing, planning, and submitting research grant applications, while helping to maximize the Chair’s outreach and knowledge mobilization activities. Funding will also be available to support doctoral scholarships associated with the Chair.
The successful candidate will join the Graduate School of International Studies as a regular faculty member beginning June 1, 2027, and no later than September 1, 2027. The appointment may be made at any academic rank, in accordance with the  Collective Agreement between Université Laval and the Syndicat des professeurs et professeures de l’Université Laval (SPUL) .
Duties include :
Teaching at the graduate level;
Supervising graduate students in the context of master’s research papers, master’s theses, and doctoral dissertations;
Conducting research and publishing on global inequalities and trade;
Participating in teaching and administrative activities associated with ESEI;
Performing any other activities provided for under the Collective Agreement between Université Laval and SPUL.

Selection Criteria :
Ability to teach in French within three years of appointment;
A PhD in a field relevant to global inequalities and trade;
Applications from doctoral candidates who are nearing completion of their dissertation will be considered, provided that a letter from the department chair confirming the expected date of dissertation submission or defence is included;
A strong commitment to multi/interdisciplinary research and teaching, demonstrated through academic training, a research program, teaching experience, or collaborations;
An excellent research record, including publications in peer-reviewed journals considered among the leading journals in the candidate’s field, or demonstrated potential to develop an excellent research record for candidates at an early career stage;
University-level teaching experience;
A record of securing research funding from granting agencies in the candidate’s field, or demonstrated potential to obtain such funding in the near future;
Ability to teach a graduate-level research methods course. Proficiency in mixed methods would be an asset;
Demonstrated involvement in national and international research networks would be an asset;
Research experience in the Global South would be an asset.

Employment Conditions :
Regular, full-time position;
Salary commensurate with recognized experience, with benefits, in accordance with the Collective Agreement between Université Laval and SPUL.

Applications :
Application deadline: October 31, 2026
Start date: June 1, 2027 (no later than September 1, 2027)

The application file must include:
A cover letter demonstrating, in particular, the candidate’s commitment to multi/interdisciplinary research and international studies;
A curriculum vitae;
A document of no more than two pages describing a five-year research chair program;
One sample of the candidate’s publications.

